{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically preferring the one that is more right-skewed. A right-skewed lottery is defined here as one where a greater portion of the probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., lower or zero payoffs) compared to favorable ones (i.e., higher payoffs). In both lotteries, the favorable outcome is the non-zero payoff (6000 or 3000 Euros), and the unfavorable outcome is 0 Euros. Lottery A has a 0.1% chance of 6000 and 99.9% chance of 0; thus, 99.9% of the probability is on the unfavorable outcome. Lottery B has 0.2% chance of 3000 and 99.8% chance of 0, so 99.8% of the probability is on the unfavorable outcome. Therefore, Lottery A is more right-skewed than Lottery B (99.9% vs 99.8% on zero). The decision reason would prefer Lottery A because it is more right-skewed. The relevant information (probabilities of favorable vs unfavorable outcomes) is fully available from the problem, so the reason can be applied.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report is in German and translates roughly to: 'Step 1: Uncover information. Step 2: Think to oneself, oh dear, very low probabilities but no loss. Step 3: Then decide for the higher gain, even though the probability is lower, because with both options there is no loss and the probability of the second outcome is very high.' The individual reports considering: (1) the very low probabilities of winning, (2) the absence of any loss (i.e., no negative outcomes), and (3) the magnitude of the potential gains. The justification for the choice is based on preferring the higher monetary outcome (6000 Euros in Lottery A), despite its lower probability, due to the safety of not facing a loss and the high likelihood of getting zero anyway. The individual focuses on the payoff size and the non-negative nature of outcomes, not on the distribution of probabilities or skewness per se.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness, specifically the concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes, and prefers the more right-skewed lottery (Lottery A). The individual does consider the high probability of zero outcomes (unfavorable) and the low probabilities of gains, which relates to the concept of skewness. However, the individual does not compare the degree of skewness between the lotteries or use it as a criterion. Instead, the justification centers on choosing the higher reward because both options are safe (no losses) and the chance of winning is very low regardless. This reflects a 'maximax' or aspiration-level heuristic, not a skewness-based rule. While there is partial overlap in considering the high chance of zero, the rule used by the individual (favoring higher gain under safety) differs from the reason’s rule (favoring greater right-skewness). Thus, it is unlikely the skewness reason was used, even though the final choice might align with it.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

{
