  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report is in German and translates to: 'Step 1: uncover information. Step 2: compare. 25% vs 20% does not make such a big difference, and since the higher loss even has the lower probability, the decision fell on Lottery B.' The individual considers the probabilities of losing (25% vs 20%) and the magnitudes of the losses (-3000 vs -4000). They note that although the loss in B is larger, it occurs with lower probability (20% vs 25%), and this seems to justify their preference for B. The justification centers on a comparison of probability and outcome size, favoring the option with the lower probability of loss despite the larger loss amount. The individual does not mention skewness, distribution shape, or any concept related to the concentration of unfavorable outcomes beyond the binary presence of a loss.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying which lottery is more right-skewed, i.e., assigns higher probability to unfavorable outcomes. As determined, Lottery A (25% loss probability) is more right-skewed than B (20%), so the reason would prefer A. However, the individual chose B and justified this by noting that the larger loss has a lower probability, which reflects a preference for lower probability of loss, possibly minimizing loss chance. This is opposite to the skewness reason, which would favor the option with higher probability of loss (if that defines right-skew in this context). The individual considered probabilities and outcomes but used them to justify avoiding loss, not to assess distribution skew. There is no indication of reasoning about the shape or skew of the distribution. The justification is inconsistent with the skewness-based rule. Therefore, it is certain that the individual did not use the skewness decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
