  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness — specifically preferring the more right-skewed lottery (i.e., one where more probability is on unfavorable outcomes). Lottery B is more right-skewed (90% chance of -3000), while Lottery A is less right-skewed (45% chance of -6000). The reason would prefer Lottery B. However, the individual chooses Lottery A, and their justification is based on preferring a lower probability of loss, even though it's larger. This suggests aversion to high-probability losses, not a preference for right-skewed distributions. The individual considers the same information (outcomes and probabilities), so there is partial overlap. But their rule — avoiding high-probability losses — contradicts the skewness reason, which favors higher probability of unfavorable outcomes. Thus, the individual’s justification opposes the reason’s rule. Therefore, it is certain that the skewness reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
