{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed (i.e., assigns more probability to unfavorable outcomes relative to favorable ones). To evaluate skewness, we need to examine the distribution of outcomes and their probabilities. Lottery A has a 25% chance of a high outcome (6000€) and a 75% chance of 0€, meaning most of the probability mass is on the low (unfavorable) outcome. Lottery B has a 25% chance of 4000€, 25% of 2000€, and 50% of 0€. Thus, it has 50% probability of a non-zero outcome and 50% of zero. Comparing skewness: A has a single high outcome with low probability and high mass on zero, making it highly right-skewed. B spreads probability across two positive outcomes, reducing its right-skew compared to A. Therefore, A is more right-skewed than B. The decision reason would prefer A because it is more right-skewed.",
